{"parentUuid":"011d936f-aeba-423a-83d4-643080984510","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"abc88b6b-0c7f-4068-93a6-b3c2a4b46c76","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013DFpD83f6y1E3iX9Cnvdea","type":"tool_result","content":"7\n49:## From a bad load to a fixed one\n51:| Step | What DataQ does |\n53:| **Detect** | A pipeline finishes and the bound suite runs, or a schedule fires. Freshness, volume, schema-drift, anomaly and \n54:| **Explain** | Each result carries the measured value, the expected range and a redacted sample of failing rows. With a bring-\n55:| **Impact** | A failing check (warn, fail or critical) on a run whose target is a known asset opens an incident on that asset,\n56:| **Gate** | A pipeline stage can ask DataQ for the verdict before it publishes. DataQ answers; your DAG decides ([Gate a pipel\n57:| **Remediate** | The owning team is alerted once, acknowledges the incident, fixes the data and re-runs the suite to confirm b\n63:## What you can do with it\n65:| | |\n67:| **Catch the incidents that page people** | Freshness, volume and schema-drift monitors notice a load that did not arrive, arr\n68:| **Author precise checks without code** | Twenty-five vetted Great Expectations types, custom SQL with a live dry-run, compari\n69:| **See the whole estate** | An asset view rolls health up per table, with lineage pulled from dbt, your catalog or the warehou\n70:| **Alert the right people, once** | Teams, Slack, email and webhooks, routed by severity, de-duplicated so a broken check repo\n71:| **Run it where the data lives** | Reference deployments on Azure Container Apps and AWS ECS, the recommended way to run DataQ\n72:| **Let assistants do the work** | Fifty-six MCP tools expose the same actions to Claude, Copilot and Cursor, every one honest \n74:## Who it is for\n80:## Quickstart\n94:## Where next","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f6a6ad0f-13bc-433b-961a-4f29fe6aeba8","timestamp":"2026-10-21T05:52:56.922Z","toolUseResult":{"st